// def-lang: a def whose body is def-lang. Its lines run one by one, like a file's lines, with A0, A1, ... put in: their values written as code, or the words as they are written for a def that keeps its arguments' words. Its value is the value of the last line that has one. The variables it sets are its own and end with it; a def that keeps its arguments' words, or an entry's `does`, runs where it acts.
// Example: twice 4, with def twice A0 whose body is A0 * 2
//          → 8 (not checked)
language def
    does in python
        ENTRY = self["entry"]
        TEXTS = ENTRY["values"] if ENTRY["as written"] else [as_code(value) for value in ENTRY["values"]]
        LINES = ENTRY["fill"](ENTRY["template"], TEXTS)
        if ENTRY["as written"] or ENTRY["in place"]:
            ENTRY["body"] = self.run_lines(LINES, ENTRY["definition"])
        else:
            with self.own_variables(ENTRY["definition"].file):
                ENTRY["body"] = self.run_lines(LINES, ENTRY["definition"])
